@kbond I closed the gap I had left open, amended into my existing commit rather than stacked on top, since it is the same "make the helpers produce URLs the listener accepts" change. Yours is still untouched.

**The problem.** `csrf_token_manager` is new in this PR, and the generator received `security.csrf.token_manager` unconditionally. A firewall setting its own manager therefore got links carrying a token minted by the default one, which its listener rejects. The failure is a 403 on a link the framework generated itself, so it is worth closing before the option ships.

**The fix.** `SecurityExtension` now registers each firewall's manager into a `security.impersonate.csrf_token_manager_locator`, keyed by firewall name, and the generator resolves the manager for the firewall it is currently building a URL for. The fifth constructor argument became that locator instead of a single manager. It is new in this PR, so there is nothing to keep compatible.

I did not copy `LogoutUrlGenerator::registerListener()`. That method exists because `LogoutUrlGenerator` has no `FirewallMap` and has to be told the whole configuration. The impersonation generator already reads `parameter`, `csrf_parameter`, `csrf_token_id` and `path` from `FirewallConfig::getSwitchUser()`, so replicating them into a listener map would create a second source of truth to keep in sync. The service instance is the only thing the config array cannot carry, since it holds a service id, so that is the only thing the locator carries. `security.user_checker_locator` a few lines above in the same extension is the precedent.

**One behaviour change.** With CSRF enabled and no manager available for the firewall, the generator now throws instead of silently returning a URL without a token. Silently omitting it produced a link that 403s at click time, which is the harder failure to diagnose. Through the extension the state is unreachable, since the listener takes a hard reference to the same service.

While there, `getFirewallConfig()` is nullable, so the unconditional `->getSwitchUser()` became `?->getSwitchUser()`: a request outside any firewall now hits the existing `LogicException` instead of a "call to a member function on null". And the changelog entry names the argument `$csrfTokenManagers`.

**Tests.** Five added, `SecurityBundle` 403 to 408, `Security/Http` unchanged at 650.

